  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch02.s09",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch02.s09.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r01",
            "text": "How can a Python program use the same kind of byte pair tokenizer as GPT-2 without reimplementing the algorithm from scratch?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r02",
            "text": "Implementing byte pair encoding from scratch is relatively complicated, so a practical approach is to use the Python open-source library \"tiktoken\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2459",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2463",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2465",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2467"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r03",
            "text": "\"tiktoken\" is essentially a byte pair encoder used for OpenAI models, and it is the tokenizer used in OpenAI models.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2472",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2474",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2476",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2479",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2581",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2584",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2586"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r04",
            "text": "To use it, the first step is to install the \"tiktoken\" library.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2502",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2504",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2590",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2592"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r05",
            "text": "After installation, the GPT-2 byte pair tokenizer is instantiated with a call such as \"tiktoken.get_encoding(\"gpt2\")\", storing the result in a tokenizer object.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2538",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2541",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2543",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2545",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2547",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2552",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2554",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2595",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2598",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2602",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2772",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2775"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p01.r06",
            "text": "That tokenizer object is similar to the earlier Python tokenizer classes because it has \"encode\" and \"decode\" methods, but here the tokenizer is created in one line through the library.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2558",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2561",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2563",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2579",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2581",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2585",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2588",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2605",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2608",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2610",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2613"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s09.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r01",
            "text": "With that tokenizer in hand, the next step is to test encoding and decoding on actual text.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r02",
            "text": "An example text is encoded with the tokenizer while allowing the special end-of-text token, and the resulting output includes token IDs such as \"50256\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@2632",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2634",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2646",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2649",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2726",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2732"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r03",
            "text": "In the GPT-2 and GPT-3 tokenization scheme, token ID \"50256\" is the end-of-text token.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2732",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2735",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2871",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2873",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2876",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2879"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r04",
            "text": "The GPT-2 and GPT-3 byte pair encoding scheme uses a vocabulary of around 50,000 tokens, and a word-level English vocabulary would require roughly 170,000 to 200,000 words.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2738",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2741",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2744",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2746",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2748",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2751",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2702",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2705",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2708",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2736",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2740"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r05",
            "text": "Byte pair encoding reduces the vocabulary size while still giving strong performance.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2753",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2757",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2759",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2766",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2768",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2770"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p02.r06",
            "text": "When the token IDs are passed to the tokenizer's \"decode\" method, the text is reconstructed exactly as it was originally given.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2820",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2822",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2828",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2831",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2834",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2836",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2843",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2846",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2751",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2753",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2755",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2757"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s09.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p03.r01",
            "text": "Two properties of the GPT-2 byte pair vocabulary are worth noting.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p03.r02",
            "text": "In the GPT-2 byte pair tokenizer, the end-of-text token is the last token in the vocabulary, so it has the largest token ID, \"50256\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2873",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2875",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2876",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2879",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2883",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2899",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2901"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p03.r03",
            "text": "The byte pair tokenizer used for GPT-2, GPT-3, and the original model used in ChatGPT has a total vocabulary size of \"50257\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2886",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2888",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2891",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2893"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p03.r04",
            "text": "A byte pair encoding tokenizer can encode and decode unknown words without any special unknown-word token because it breaks text down to characters and subwords.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2907",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2909",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2912",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2919",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2921",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2932",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2934",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2937",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2939",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2944",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2946",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2949",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2951",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2962",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2965",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2967",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2969",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2621",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2623",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2627",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2630",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2641",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2651"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p03.r05",
            "text": "For an unknown word, the tokenizer would ideally produce an error, but with the byte pair tokenizer no error occurs.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@2637",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2639",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2641",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2646",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2649",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2651"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch02.s09.p04",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p04.r01",
            "text": "This becomes even clearer on deliberately made-up words.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p04.r02",
            "text": "A final example uses the random nonsense string \"a k w i r w i e r\", described as completely random words that do not make sense.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2979",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2981",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2983",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2985",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2987",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2991",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2993"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p04.r03",
            "text": "When these random words are passed to the \"tiktoken\" GPT-2 byte pair encoder, it does not raise an error and still encodes them into tokens.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2993",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2995",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@2997",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3000",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3002",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3005"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p04.r04",
            "text": "Decoding the resulting integers gives back exactly the same sentence that was originally encoded.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3055",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3058",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3060",
              "fKd8s29e-l4@3062"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch02.s09.p04.r05",
            "text": "So the tokenizer created with \"tiktoken.get_encoding(\"gpt2\")\" can be tested with both \"encode\" and \"decode\".",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "mk-6cFebjis@2772",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2775",
              "mk-6cFebjis@2777"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
